ase) | • **Tension‑type** – muscle‑fiber tension, peripheral nociceptor activation in scalp/neck muscles.<br>• **Migraine** – cortical spreading depression, trigeminovascular activation, release of vasoactive neuropeptides (e.g., CGRP) → transient dilation of meningeal vessels and neurogenic inflammation.<br>• **Cluster / trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias** – hypothalamic dysregulation, parasympathetic activation causing lacrimation, nasal congestion. | • Daily or episodic “pressing” pain, often bilateral (tension).<br>• Throbbing, unilateral pain with nausea, photophobia, aura (migraine).<br>• Severe unilateral orbital pain with tearing, nasal congestion (cluster). |\n| **Secondary headaches** (symptom of another condition) | • **Vascular** – subarachnoid hemorrhage, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, arterial dissection.<br>• **Infectious** – meningitis, encephalitis, sinusitis, dental infection.<br>• **Neoplastic** – primary brain tumor, metastasis (often progressive, worse when lying down).<br>• **Traumatic** – post‑concussive headache, CSF leak after lumbar puncture or spinal anesthesia (orthostatic headache).<br>• **Metabolic / systemic** – hypertension crisis, hypoxia, anemia, thyroid disease, electrolyte imbalance.<br>• **Medication‑related** – overuse of analgesics, withdrawal from caffeine, nitroglycerin, vasoactive drugs.<br>• **Other** – giant cell arteritis, intracranial pressure changes, hormonal fluctuations (menstrual migraine). | • Sudden “worst‑ever” thunderclap headache (subarachnoid bleed).<br>• Headache with fever, neck stiffness (meningitis).<br>• Progressive daily headache with vomiting, visual changes (possible tumor).<br>• Headache that improves when lying flat and worsens upright after spinal tap (CSF leak). |\n\n*The historical PDF you provided mentions migraine pathophysiology (electrical/chemical imbalance → vessel constriction then dilation) and a post‑spinal‑anesthetic orthostatic headache, both of which remain consistent with current understanding, but the text is dated (2002) and does not reflect newer concepts such as CGRP‑targeted therapies or updated red‑flag criteria.*\n\n---\n\n## 2.  How clinicians usually approach a new headache  \n\n1. **History – “red‑flag” questions**  \n   - Onset: sudden (“thunderclap”) vs. gradual?  \n   - Age of first onset (new headache after age\u202f50 is a red flag).  \n   - Pattern change: worsening frequency/intensity?  \n   - Associated symptoms: fever, neck stiffness, rash, visual loss, focal weakness, seizures, confusion, vomiting, weight loss, night sweats.  \n   - Triggers/relieving factors: posture (orthostatic), Valsalva, alcohol, sleep, stress.  \n   - Medication use: daily analgesics, recent new drugs, caffeine withdrawal.  \n   - Past medical history: hypertension, cancer, clotting disorders, recent head/neck trauma, recent spinal procedures.  \n\n2. **Physical exam**  \n   - Vital signs (especially blood pressure, fever).  \n   - Neurologic exam (cranial nerves, motor/sensory, coordination, gait).  \n   - Neck stiffness, papilledema, temporal artery tenderness.  \n\n3. **When to seek urgent care / call emergency services**  \n   - Sudden severe (“worst ever”) headache.  \n   - New headache with fever, neck stiffness, altered mental status.  \n   - Focal neurologic deficits (weakness, speech change, vision loss).  \n   - Headache with trauma and loss of consciousness.  \n   - Headache with signs of increased intracranial pressure (vomiting, papilledema).  \n   - Suspected giant cell arteritis (new temporal headache in >50\u202fy with jaw claudication, visual symptoms).  \n\n4. **Typical next steps for non‑emergent headaches**  \n   - If primary (tension‑type, migraine, cluster) and no red flags → trial of acute therapy (e.g., NSAID, triptan for migraine) and lifestyle measures.  \n   - If secondary cause suspected → imaging (CT head non‑contrast for acute bleed; MRI/MRA for vascular or neoplastic concerns) and/or labs (CBC, ESR/CRP, metabolic panel).  \n   - Consider referral to neurology or headache specialist for refractory or atypical patterns.  \n\n---\n\n## 3.  Common primary headache triggers (often modifiable)\n\n| Trigger | Typical effect |\n|---------|----------------|\n| **Stress / muscle tension** | Increases scalp/neck muscle tone → tension‑type headache. |\n| **Sleep deprivation / irregular schedule** | Lowers pain threshold, can precipitate migraine. |\n| **Hormonal changes** (menstruation, pregnancy, menopause) | Fluctuations in estrogen can trigger migraine. |\n| **Dietary factors** (caffeine, alcohol, tyramine‑rich foods, nitrites) | Can provoke migraine or cluster attacks. |\n| **Environmental** (bright light, loud noise, strong odors) | Photophobia/phonophobia in migraine; can worsen tension headache. |\n| **Medication overuse** (≥10\u202fdays/month of analgesics) | Leads to medication‑overuse headache. |\n| **Weather/altitude changes** | May affect vascular tone, especially in migraine. |\n\n---\n\n## 4.  When to follow up with a clinician  \n\n- **New or changed headache pattern** (especially after age\u202f50).  \n- **Any red‑flag symptom** listed above.  \n- **Headache that interferes with daily activities** despite OTC treatment.  \n- **Headache that wakes you from sleep** or is **worse when lying down** (possible increased intracranial pressure).  \n- **Persistent daily headache** >\u202f4\u202fweeks without clear cause.  \n\n---\n\n## 5.  Key take‑away points  \n\n1. **Headaches are common** and most are benign primary types, but they can also signal serious disease.  \n2. **Ask about red‑flag features** and seek emergency care if they are present.  \n3. **Identify triggers** and consider lifestyle or pharmacologic strategies for primary headaches.  \n4. **If you are uncertain** or the headache is new, worsening, or associated with systemic/neurologic signs, **schedule a medical evaluation**.  \n\n---\n\n### References (current, reputable sources)\n\n1. **American Headache Society – C